             kind "digit" with data.value 10..15 (g_ascii_xdigit_value, so the
             decimal pads are unaffected) -- a downstream accumulator folds a
             key in with acc * 16 + value. "C" is twelve here, NOT the
             calculator pad's clear key; the kind tells them apart.
       [x] 76.8.3 "Phone Keyboard": the decimal grid key for key, with the
             telephone letter groups under the digits (2 = ABC .. 9 = WXYZ).
             Lettered keys add data.letters; 1, 0, * and # stay bare. 188 px
             wide (vs the decimal pad's 152) so "PQRS" fits under the 7.
       [x] 76.8.4 The painter draws a sublabel as a smaller second line,
             centred with the digit as one block and blended 40% toward the
             key face so the digit still reads first.
       [x] 76.8.5 Help page, pipnode-nodes skill entry, and unit tests: the hex
             table + its values, the phone pad matching the decimal one key for
             key, letters present/absent, the enum nicks, and keys-disjoint /
             hit-test now run over ALL FOUR layouts.
       [ ] 76.8.6 GUI check by hand: the hex pad's A-F in the accent colour,
             and the phone pad's letter groups legible under the digits.

[ ] 77 Burglar Alarm node (PnBurglarAlarm) -- the first CONTROLLER: a
       self-contained state machine that runs a whole appliance rather than
       transforming one message. Five inputs that mean different things and five
       outputs that speak at different times, which is what sets the new
       "Controllers" palette group apart from the Filters groups. Keypad ->
       Panel -> LCD + siren + voice + radio is a working intruder alarm.

       [x] 77.1 Core lib/pn-burglar-alarm.[ch] (GTK-free, no gui tier -- the node
             is header-only and the six properties render in the generic
             settings dialog). Logic seam pn_burglar_alarm_press() / _submit() /
             _set_zone() / _trip_zone() / _tick() so a headless test can walk a
             whole burglary without building messages or waiting out a real
             delay. _tick() deliberately does NOT touch the node's own 1 Hz
             timer, so a test driving it directly never arms a main-loop source.
       [x] 77.2 Inputs: 0 keypad (data.key, one keystroke -- what the Keypad's
             Decimal pad emits; failing that a whole code in data.output, so
             MQTT can arm remotely), 1 delayed (the entry/exit route), 2..4
             zone 1-3 (instant). A numeric data.value sets a zone's HELD state
             (a door contact); NO numeric value is a MOMENTARY trip (a PIR) that
             leaves the zone closed -- the difference is what decides whether
             the panel can arm.
       [x] 77.3 Zone names ARE the input names (pn_node_get_input_name), so a
             rename on the node's Inputs tab renames the zone in the readout,
             the spoken sentence and the sms. No bespoke name properties.
       [x] 77.4 Outputs: 0 alarm (the siren, 0.0/1.0 + data.cause), 1 display
             (2-4 LCD lines in data.output, countdown seconds in data.value),
             2 armed (0.0/1.0), 3 speech (one spoken sentence per event, for
             Text to Speech), 4 sms (one written sentence per IMPORTANT event,
             for Meshtastic). Every message carries data.state. alarm/armed are
             written only on a change; sms on arming is opt-in (a radio message
             per arming is a lot of radio).
       [x] 77.5 States disarmed -> exit -> armed -> entry -> alarm. Arming is
             refused while an INSTANT zone is held open (NOT READY names it);
             the delayed zone may be open -- that is the door being left
             through. Zones are ignored during the exit delay but re-read the
             moment arming bites. The siren cuts off after siren-time while the
             state STAYS alarm, remembering the cause, until a code disarms.
       [x] 77.6 The panel paints itself red on the worksheet while tripped, via
             the generic node error state (TODO #71-era pn_node_set_has_error)
             rather than a bespoke icon swap.
       [x] 77.7 Startup announce (g_idle, Knob/Switch pattern): display, armed=0
             and alarm=0 once on load, so a wired LCD is not left blank and a
             wired siren is told to be quiet -- but SILENT on speech and sms,
             which would otherwise talk to the room every time the worksheet is
             opened. The state itself is not serialized: a reloaded worksheet
             comes up disarmed, the only safe assumption about a house nobody
             was watching.
       [x] 77.8 Matrix57 grew from 1-2 to 1-4 lines so the display output has a
             real 20x4 target (the request was "a two line or a four line LCD").
             Past two rows the NODE grows (PN_M57_ROW_HEIGHT, 45 px per extra
             row) instead of the dots shrinking; 1 and 2 keep the historical
             90 px body so no existing worksheet moves. The panel-applet mirror
             follows the same range but keeps its panel-row height.
       [x] 77.9 Factory registration under the new "Controllers" category, help
             page PnBurglarAlarm.html, pipnode-nodes skill entry (the new
             reference/controllers.md + the Matrix57 line-count update).
       [x] 77.10 Unit tests tests/unit/test-pn-burglar-alarm.c -- the whole
             state machine driven through the logic seam (126 checks): exit and
             entry delays, zero-delay shortcuts, wrong/empty codes, NOT READY,
             held vs momentary zones, the siren cut-off and its no-cut-off mode,
             the 2/3/4-line readouts, the startup announce (and its silence on
             speech/sms), sms gating, and the message shapes on all five
             outputs.
       [x] 77.11 Example worksheet examples/controls-and-logic/burglar-alarm.json
             (Keypad + four Switches -> Panel -> 20x4 Matrix57, two LEDs, Text to
             Speech, and a Text View standing in for a Meshtastic node).
       [ ] 77.12 GUI check by hand: type 1234 # on the pad and watch the LCD
             count the exit delay down to ARMED; flip the Front door switch and
             confirm the entry delay counts instead of the siren; flip a window
             and confirm the siren LED blinks, the node paints red, and the
