       [x] 80.16 DECIDED (2026-09-21): ANIMATION -- when an argument
             resolves to a VECTOR the figure becomes a film. Frame i
             takes element i of every vector; scalars broadcast
             unchanged; the frame count is the SHORTEST vector INPUT
             the program reads, and that is on purpose, NOT because
             the arithmetic truncates -- it does not (80.3f). Every
             frame is drawn from real values of every input; the
             reasoning is in TODO #82.1.
             REOPENED (2026-09-22) and RE-DECIDED (2026-09-26, TODO
             #82.1): the original parenthesis claimed vector OP vector
             truncates to the shorter operand, which was a misreading
             of PnVarStore -- see the correction in 80.3(f). Shortest
             won again, for the reason now given above.
             The whole animation strand left this entry with it: #80
             closes on the still figure, and #82 is where the film is
             built.

             (a) EVALUATE ONCE FOR THE WHOLE FILM, INDEX AT PAINT TIME.
                 The obvious implementation -- re-run the program per
                 frame with each vector variable replaced by its i-th
                 element -- is both slower and WRONG-ER than doing
                 nothing: PnVarStore is already elementwise, so
                 evaluating `dx = 50 * cos(a)` with a vector `a` simply
                 yields a vector dx. So the resolved display list holds
                 values that may be vectors, and the frame index is
                 applied where the painter reads them. One evaluation
                 per input change, not per frame; scrubbing costs
                 nothing; the timer only repaints.
             (b) WHICH MAKES THE WHOLE THING FALL OUT OF THE EXISTING
                 SEMANTICS rather than being bolted on. A Ramp into a
                 Calculator into a Figure animates with no new
                 plumbing -- the same pipeline the Compute examples
                 already build.
             (c) A VECTOR IN A PEN-STATE ARGUMENT ANIMATES TOO, for
                 free: `color` fading through the film, `width`
                 thickening, even `view` panning, all from the same
                 indexing. No special cases.
             (d) THE 80.10(c) ERROR CLASS DISAPPEARS HERE, exactly as
                 predicted: "a vector reached a scalar sink" stops
                 being a wiring mistake and becomes the frame indexer.
                 That seam was put there for this.
             (e) A comparison still collapses a vector to one scalar
                 (80.3f), so `hot = s > 0` cannot vary per frame. Say
                 so in the help and point at mask arithmetic.
             (f) 80.12's dump grows a frame argument -- dump frame i --
                 so the tests can assert a whole film, not just a
                 still.
       [x] 80.17 DECIDED (2026-09-21): animation controls.

             (a) PROPERTIES: `fps` (default 25, capped at 60),
                 `play-mode` (once / loop / ping-pong -- "hold last" is
                 what once already does, so three modes, not four) and
                 `frames`, an explicit frame count where 0 means "take
                 it from the data".
             (b) THE TIMER LIVES IN THE CORE HALF, so the behaviour
                 belongs to the node and not to the painter: a
                 g_timeout at 1000/fps advances the index and calls
                 pn_node_request_repaint(). Stopped in dispose(), never
                 started when the frame count is 1, and the index
                 resets to 0 when new data arrives -- a new film starts
                 at the beginning.
             (c) DO NOT SPIN A TIMER NOBODY IS WATCHING. Headless, a
                 figure has no painter and advancing an index forever
                 is pure waste. The node can tell: nothing is connected
                 to its "repaint-needed" signal, so no listener means
                 no timer. Confirm the signal-handler check behaves at
                 implementation time; the rule stands either way.
             (d) `frame` AND `t` COLLIDE WITH 80.16(a), and this is the
                 real content of this sub-point. If the program is
                 evaluated ONCE for the whole film, a per-frame scalar
                 variable cannot exist. The fix is to make them
                 vectors: bind `frame` as [0..N-1] and `t` as
                 [0..1], so they animate through the very same
                 indexing as everything else, with no re-evaluation and
                 no special case in the painter.
             (e) WHICH LEAVES ONE CHICKEN-AND-EGG: N comes from the
                 data, but `t` is part of the data. Two answers, and
                 both are cheap. When `frames` > 0 the count is
                 explicit, `t` and `frame` are bound up front, and a
                 figure with NO inputs at all can still animate -- a
                 turning wheel needs no Ramp. When `frames` is 0 we
                 need a second evaluation pass to learn N first, and
                 80.2 rule 12's free-identifier walker tells us for
                 nothing whether the program even mentions `t` or
                 `frame`, so the second pass runs only for the programs
                 that need it. At most two evaluations, only on input
                 change.
       [x] 80.18 DECIDED (2026-09-21): the repeat-versus-frames
             tension, settled before 80.16 was coded rather than after,
             as promised. A vector means FRAMES, so what draws twenty
             tick marks along an axis?

             (a) THE FORM, if and when it is built: an explicit
                 `repeat n` ... `end` block binding a scalar index
                 variable. NOT a one-statement prefix (our arguments
                 are comma-separated expressions, so a prefix needs a
                 separator character the language does not otherwise
                 have), and emphatically NOT letting a vector expand
                 into many shapes, which would make the same value mean
                 two different things depending on where it appeared.
             (b) THE INVARIANT THAT KEEPS THE TWO IDEAS APART, and it
                 is the whole reason to decide this now: INSIDE a
                 repeat, a vector is STILL a frame, never an iteration,
                 and the loop index is always a scalar. Write that in
                 the help the day the block lands.
             (c) NOT IN V1. The actual demand -- axis ticks, hatching,
                 a grating's slits -- is better served by the dedicated
                 helpers already queued in 80.19 (`axes`, hatching),
                 which draw the right thing in one statement instead of
                 making everyone hand-roll a loop. A gear's teeth would
                 genuinely want the block; when something real asks for
                 it, build (a).
       [ ] 80.19 Later primitives, in the order a physics plate wants
             them: arrows (a figure of forces without arrowheads is not a
             figure of forces), ROTATED LABELS along an axis or a ray
             (deferred out of 80.7(h), and the first of these anyone
             will miss), angle-mark arcs with a label, dimension lines
             with ticks, hatching for a wall or a ground plane, bezier
             curves, and an `axes` helper that draws a labelled pair of
             axes in one statement. Each one is a verb plus a
             paragraph in the help page; none of them changes the engine.
       [ ] 80.20 Export, if it ever earns its place: the display list is
             backend-agnostic, so the same walk that drives cairo could
             emit SVG for a figure someone wants in a document, or a PNG
             sequence for an animated one. Note it and move on -- v1
             ships without it.
       [ ] 80.21 GUI check by hand, at the end: wind the input knobs and
             watch the figure track them; drag a Figure into the zoom
             overlay and confirm it scales cleanly (vector drawing, so it
             must); break the program mid-edit and confirm the node turns
             red with a readable line number instead of drawing garbage;
             then wire a vector in and watch it move.
       [ ] 80.22 THE CODE, part 1 of 4: the front end. lib/pn-figure.c,
             core tier -- everything that turns the `program` string
             into a display list, and nothing that evaluates or draws.
             NOTE ON NUMBERING: 80.22-80.25 are numbered after the
             design sub-points because this plan was written
             decision-first; in BUILD order they come straight after
             80.11 and before 80.12, and nothing from 80.12 onwards can
             start until they exist. Contents:
             [x] 80.22.1 DONE (2026-09-21): the line scanner --
                   comment stripping that knows about strings,
                   trailing-comma continuation, blank lines, leading
                   whitespace (80.2 rules 4, 5, 6). pn_figure_scan()
                   in lib/pn-figure.c, with PnFigureError (the
                   collector 80.22.6 will format) and PnFigureLine.
                   Three things the rules did not already settle, all
                   decided here:
                   (a) A logical line is not just a string, because an
                       error in the tail of a continued `poly` must
                       still name the line the tail was TYPED on. So
                       it carries the PIECES it was joined from, and
                       pn_figure_line_locate() maps an offset in the
                       joined text back to a source line and column --
                       which is what hands 80.22.5 and 80.22.6 the
                       position the expression parser reports as a
                       bare "position %d". Pieces join with no
                       separator, unambiguous because a join only ever
                       happens straight after a comma.
                   (b) INSIDE a continuation a comment-only line keeps
                       it going (a long `poly` wants commenting from
                       the inside) but a truly BLANK line ends it,
                       leaving the dangling comma in the text for
                       80.22.2 to complain about. Swallowing the next
                       statement instead would hide the mistake.
                   (c) An unterminated string -- the one error the
                       scanner can find, since it tracks quotes to
                       find comments at all -- reports at the OPENING
                       quote and DROPS the whole logical line, so no
                       later stage re-reports the same broken quoting
                       and inflates 80.2 rule 9's count.
                   Columns count characters, not bytes, so a message
                   points at the right place after a non-ASCII string
                   literal earlier on the line. Wired into
                   lib/Makefile.am (core sources and core headers) and
                   pipnode.h; tests/unit/test-pn-figure.c exists with
                   eleven scanner cases and is in both Makefile.am
                   lists, ready for 80.12 to grow the dump cases into.
             [x] 80.22.2 DONE (2026-09-21): statement splitting --
                   verb-or-assignment on one lookahead, commas at
                   paren depth 0 outside quotes, string literals with
                   their escapes (80.2 rules 1, 3). pn_figure_split()
                   in lib/pn-figure.c, producing a PnFigureStatement
                   per line with PnFigureArg arguments that are each
                   already EXPRESSION or STRING -- rule 7 settled
                   once, so no later stage asks again. Each argument
                   keeps its offset in the joined line text, and the
                   statement borrows the PnFigureLine, which is what
                   turns 80.22.5's "position %d" into a place a person
                   can look at. What this sub-point had to settle:
                   (a) `\n` IS AN ESCAPE, which the grammar in 80.2
                       did not list but 80.7(d) requires, since a
                       newline splits a label into lines. So the set
                       is `\"`, `\\` and `\n`, and any OTHER escape
                       is an error rather than a literal backslash --
                       a typo should be loud, and the help page has
                       three escapes to document instead of a rule
                       about what happens to the rest.
                   (b) A STRING IS THE WHOLE ARGUMENT OR IT IS A
                       MISTAKE: `"A" + 1` is an error pointing at the
                       `+`, not an expression with a stray quote in
                       it. The expression lexer would have said
                       "unexpected character" and been technically
                       right, which is not the same as useful.
                   (c) The dangling comma 80.22.1(b) deliberately left
                       in the text arrives here as an EMPTY ARGUMENT,
                       reported at the comma. Same error for
                       `line 1,,2`, which is the same mistake.
                   (d) A line that fails is left OUT of the statement
                       list and the split carries on, so the errors on
                       the lines after it are still found (80.2 rule
                       9). Nothing draws when any error exists, so a
                       missing statement costs nothing.
                   A stray ')' does not send the depth negative: it is
                   clamped at 0 so the commas after it still split, and
                   the expression parser gets to give the real message.
             [x] 80.22.3 DONE (2026-09-22): the verb table -- name,
                   arity, argument kinds -- covering 80.5, 80.6 and
                   80.7, with the two-spelling colour argument as the
                   single exception (80.5). All twenty verbs, one
                   static table in lib/pn-figure.c behind
                   pn_figure_check_verbs(), which fills in a
                   PnFigureVerb enum on each statement so no later
                   stage ever strcmps a verb name again.
                   (a) THE TABLE SHAPE that fits every verb but the
                       colour pair: a min and a max count, a `kinds`
                       string giving the required kind per position
                       ('e' expression, 's' string) and a `tail` kind
                       for every position past it. `text` is then
                       "ees" + tail 'e' -- x, y, format, then one
                       expression per conversion -- and `poly`/`path`
                       are tail 'e' with an even-count flag. Nothing
                       needed a per-verb function.
                   (b) COLOUR IS CHECKED SEPARATELY, because it is the
                       one place where the argument COUNT decides what
                       the arguments MEAN: 1 quoted literal, or 3-4
                       expressions, and nothing in between.
                   (c) MESSAGES ARE PLACED, NOT DESCRIBED. A count
                       that is wrong reports at the verb and names it
                       ("line takes 4 arguments, not 3"); a kind that
                       is wrong reports at the ARGUMENT, so it needs
                       no ordinal ("expected a quoted string"). The
                       one-argument colour form is the exception and
                       says "expected a quoted colour or 3 or 4
                       numbers", because someone who wrote `color c`
                       needs telling that the other spelling exists,
                       not that a string was wanted.
                   (d) An unknown verb is quoted AS TYPED, not as
                       folded, so a mistyped `Circel` reads back the
                       way it was written.
                   (e) A statement that fails is REMOVED from the
                       list, the rest still checked. Nothing after
                       this stage should have to ask whether a verb is
                       real.
                   NOT done here, deliberately: what a literal MEANS.
                   `color "banana"` and `dash "wiggly"` pass this
                   stage and fail 80.22.4, which is the sub-point that
                   owns pn_color_parse and the dash names.
             [x] 80.22.4 DONE (2026-09-22): literal parsing at PARSE
                   time -- colours through pn_color_parse (80.5a),
                   dash styles, alignment words, and printf format
                   validation with its conversion count (80.7c). All
                   of these are errors the user sees before the figure
                   ever runs. pn_figure_parse_literals() in
                   lib/pn-figure.c, storing what it read on the
                   argument itself: a PnColor, or the word the literal
                   names, or -- for a format -- how many values it
                   wants. Needed 80.5(b)'s colour-name table first,
                   which landed in its own commit as that sub-point
                   required.
                   (a) A CLOSED VOCABULARY IS WORTH SPELLING OUT. The
                       dash and alignment messages name every word
                       that belongs there (expected "solid", "dot",
                       "dash" or "dashdot") rather than reporting that
                       this one is unknown. It matters most for
                       `align "middle"`, where the word is real and
                       only in the wrong place, and the useful message
                       is which axis takes what.
                       Colour has no closed vocabulary, so it keeps
                       the plain "unknown colour" form.
                   (b) `align` TAKES BOTH SPELLINGS OF CENTRE, for the
                       same reason pn-color.c takes both grey and
                       gray: the word is unavoidable and the spelling
                       is not worth an error message.
                   (c) THE FORMAT CHECK IS SAFETY. Flags, width and
                       precision are allowed; a `*` width is not,
                       because it would eat one of the values, and a
                       length modifier is not, because it would change
                       the argument's type. A trailing `%` reads as an
                       unfinished conversion and reports as one. The
                       NUL check has to come BEFORE the strchr of
                       allowed conversion letters, or strchr finds its
                       own terminator and lets `"%"` through -- the
                       classic trap, and a real one here.
                   (d) The count mismatch reports at the FORMAT, not
                       at the values, because the format is the thing
                       to go and count.
                   Only the one-argument colour spelling has a literal
                   to read; the 3-4 expression form is evaluated per
                   frame like any other argument.
             [x] 80.22.5 DONE (2026-09-22): per-argument expression
                   parsing into ASTs, plus the free-identifier walk,
                   plus constant folding of any argument that has none
                   (80.3b, 80.2 rule 12). pn_figure_parse_expressions()
                   and pn_figure_free_names() in lib/pn-figure.c. The
                   specimen at the head of this entry parses whole,
                   with three names and thirteen statements, and that
                   is a test.
                   (a) THE PARSER'S POSITION GOES IN THE COLUMN, not in
                       the text. Its messages end in " at position N"
                       counted within the fragment; that tail is split
                       off and folded into the argument's own offset,
                       so the reader gets one position instead of two
                       that disagree. A message without a tail (there
                       are a few) falls back on the argument's own
                       place -- never wrong, only less precise.
                   (b) `pi` AND `e` FOLD LIKE NUMBERS and never appear
                       in the collected names, because they are the
                       language's constants and not something the
                       program expects from outside. One table in
                       pn-figure.c, used by the folder and the walker,
                       which #81 deletes (80.3d).
                   (c) AN ASSIGNMENT NEVER FOLDS even when its value is
                       constant: 80.2 rule 13 clears the store every
                       frame, so the binding has to happen every frame
                       too.
                   (d) THE NAME LIST KEEPS NAMES THE PROGRAM ASSIGNS,
                       when it also reads them. Subtracting them would
                       read better in a dialog and would BREAK the
                       promise that an unwired figure draws: a program
                       that reads `dx` on the line above the one that
                       assigns it must get 0, not an evaluation
                       failure.
                   (e) A CONSTANT ARGUMENT IS EVALUATED NOW, so the one
                       way it can fail -- a function that does not
                       exist -- is a parse-time error with a line and
                       column. A non-finite result is NOT an error
                       (80.10b): 1/0 folds to inf and the resolver will
                       skip that statement.
                   (f) atan2(1,2) REPORTS WELL ALREADY, which is worth
                       knowing since #81 is about that gap: our
                       splitter keeps the call whole (its comma is at
                       paren depth 1), and the calculator's lexer then
                       meets a comma it has no token for -- "unexpected
                       character ','", pointed straight at the comma.
             [x] 80.22.6 DONE (2026-09-22): error collection -- every
                   error, reported as the first plus a count, with line
                   and column (80.2 rule 9, 80.10a). Collection itself
                   was built into every stage from 80.22.1 onwards, so
                   what this sub-point added is
                   pn_figure_errors_to_string(): the text 80.10(f)'s
                   read-only `error` property will carry, one line for
                   a single error and a second line with the count when
                   there are several, in rule 9's own words.
                   (a) "FIRST" MEANS EARLIEST IN THE PROGRAM, not first
                       in the list, and the two really do differ: an
                       unterminated string on line 5 is found by the
                       scanner before a bad verb on line 2 is found by
                       the verb table. A person reads their program top
                       to bottom, so the report is scanned for the
                       smallest (line, column) rather than sorted --
                       sorting would disturb an order the stages and
                       their tests rely on.
                   (b) ONE STATEMENT YIELDS AT MOST ONE ERROR, found
                       while writing the tests rather than designed:
                       each stage gives up on a statement at its first
                       bad argument, and a failed statement is removed
                       before the next stage sees it. So the count is
                       the number of BROKEN STATEMENTS, and no program
                       can put two errors on one line -- which is why
                       the column tie-break is tested against the
                       formatter directly instead of through a
                       program.
       [x] 80.23 DONE (2026-09-22): THE CODE, part 2 of 4: the back
             end. Still lib/pn-figure.c, still core, still nothing that
             draws. pn_figure_resolve() runs one frame into a
             device-space display list and
             pn_figure_display_to_string() prints it; twenty-two cases
             in tests/unit/test-pn-figure.c, valgrind clean, and the
             specimen from the head of #80 resolves into the node's own
             280x173 client area with every number checked by hand.
             [x] 80.23.1 DONE: the binding snapshot (#PnFigureSnapshot)
                   and the per-frame cycle, with pi and e pre-bound
                   (80.3c, 80.3d).
                   (a) THE CLEAR IS A NEW STORE. 80.2 rule 13 asks for
                       pn_var_store_clear() before every frame;
                       building the store inside pn_figure_resolve()
                       and dropping it at the end says the same thing
                       and cannot forget. Last frame's assignments
                       cannot leak into this one if they were never
                       there. One GObject per repaint, at a 100 ms
                       floor, is not a cost worth designing around.
                   (b) THE SNAPSHOT HOLDS PnExprValue, vectors
                       included, and objects to none of them: a vector
                       is an error only where it reaches an ARGUMENT,
                       which is the one place a message can name the
                       line it went wrong on (80.10c). A vector may
                       therefore be bound, assigned and never read
                       without hurting anybody.
                   (c) ZERO-FILL SKIPS WHAT THE SNAPSHOT HAS, which is
                       the whole of 80.3(c)'s precedence in one `if`:
                       an input beats the zero-fill, an assignment
                       beats the input because it runs later, and the
                       constants come first because pi silently
                       becoming 0 is the worst kind of bug.
             [x] 80.23.2 DONE: the pen-state machine over the whole
                   state vector of 80.5, reset to defaults every frame.
                   (a) THE STATE IS KEPT IN USER UNITS and resolved to
                       device units as it is emitted, which is the only
                       reading of `width 2` that survives a `view`
                       halfway down the program. So a `view` that
                       CHANGES THE SCALE re-emits the state that
                       depended on it -- width, dash, font -- and the
                       dump shows it happening. The alternative,
                       resolving at use time, would have put the
                       conversion in the painter, which is exactly what
                       80.4(i) is trying to keep out of it.
                   (b) THE FRAME OPENS WITH THE WHOLE PEN STATE in the
                       display list -- colour, nofill, width, dash,
                       font, align. It is not padding: it is 80.5(g)'s
                       reset made visible, and it means the painter
                       holds no defaults of its own, so a figure cannot
                       inherit a colour or a width from the frame
                       before it even by accident. Seven lines of
                       preamble on every dump is the price, and the
                       tests spell it once in a macro.
                   (c) THE DEFAULTS, which 80.5 left unsaid: stroke
                       black, fill black but OFF, width 1 user unit,
                       solid, align centre/middle (80.7g), pen at the
                       user origin (80.6a), the window of 80.4(c). Font
                       size 5 USER UNITS -- a label about a twentieth
                       of the default hundred-unit window high, which
                       is what a plate's labels are.
                   (d) `align "left"` LEAVES THE VERTICAL AXIS ALONE
                       rather than resetting it, because that is what
                       pen state means: one verb moves one thing.
             [x] 80.23.3 DONE: the view transform of 80.4, including
                   letterboxing, reversed bounds and the skip rule for
                   a degenerate window.
                   (a) THE SIGNED SCALES MAKE REVERSED BOUNDS FREE, as
                       80.4(d) said they would: sx and sy carry the
                       direction, and nothing downstream has a special
                       case. `stretch` is two scales instead of one and
                       is the same three lines.
                   (b) ARC ANGLES ARE RESOLVED IN CORE, which was not
                       planned and is plainly right. 80.6(d) says the
                       painter calls cairo_arc_negative with negated
                       angles -- true for the ordinary view and WRONG
                       under a reversed bound, where one flip cancels
                       the other. Core knows the signs, so core maps
                       the angle (device = k * user + phi, k and phi
                       from the two signs) and the operation carries
                       @negative, which says plainly which of
                       cairo_arc() and cairo_arc_negative() to call.
                       The map is LINEAR on purpose: going through
                       atan2() would wrap 360 degrees to 0 and turn a
                       full circle into nothing.
                   (c) A DEGENERATE CLIENT AREA is not an error either
                       -- zero width or height returns an empty list
                       and no message, since a card mid-animation is
                       briefly that shape.
             [x] 80.23.4 DONE: resolution into a device-space display
                   list, with the three error classes of 80.10 and the
                   evaluate-fully-then-paint contract of 80.10(d).
                   (a) THE VERBS COLLAPSE ON THE WAY IN: `lineto`,
                       `rline` and `line` are one LINE operation
                       between two device points, `rmove` is an
                       absolute MOVE, and a `circle` is an ARC's
                       simpler cousin. Resolving them is the back end's
                       job precisely so the painter never has to know
                       the pen is in user units.
                   (b) AN ASSIGNMENT LEAVES NO OPERATION. It changes a
                       variable, not the drawing, and its effect is
                       already in the numbers of the statements that
                       follow it. The dump is the display list, not the
                       program.
                   (c) TWO SKIP REASONS, not one: "non-finite" for a
                       NaN or an infinity anywhere in the arguments,
                       and "degenerate" for a value that had to be
                       positive and was not -- a zero radius, a zero
                       font size, a negative width, a dash scale of 0,
                       a window with no extent. Both are 80.10(b) and
                       neither paints the node red.
                   (d) THE FINITE CHECK COMES FIRST, before any verb
                       looks at its arguments, so `circle 0,0,1/0` is
                       reported as non-finite rather than as a
                       degenerate radius. One rule, checked in one
                       place.
                   (e) ONE FAILED STATEMENT EMPTIES THE LIST, which is
                       what makes 80.10(d)'s promise honest: the
                       statements after it are not run and the ones
                       before it are thrown away, so "nothing is drawn"
                       means nothing.
             [x] 80.23.5 DONE: the dump, written WITH the resolver, in
                   the format 80.12 pins down -- which is the only way
                   to see what the resolver did, and it found two of
                   the bullets above.
                   (a) IT IS TWO FUNCTIONS, NOT pn_figure_dump() YET.
                       That name takes a PnFigure, and the GObject is
                       80.24's; the seam here is pn_figure_resolve()
                       plus pn_figure_display_to_string(), which the
                       node's pn_figure_dump() will be four lines on
                       top of. Splitting them also lets the painter
                       take the list the tests assert on, rather than a
                       string.
                   (b) DEVICE NUMBERS AT %.2f, INCLUDING THE VIEW'S
                       DEVICE BOX -- 80.12(b)'s sketch writes that box
                       as "rect 0 0 100 100", but it is a device
                       rectangle like every other and gets the same two
                       decimals. The WINDOW keeps %g, because that is
                       what the program typed. A negative zero, and a
                       coordinate that lands a hair under zero, print
                       as "0.00": the same place, and only one of them
                       can be an expected string.
                   (c) AN ARC PRINTS "negative" OR "positive", not
                       "clockwise": clockwise would have to say
                       clockwise IN WHICH SPACE, and that is 80.6(d)'s
                       whole trap. The words are the cairo calls.
                   (d) THE TEXT IS FILLED IN THROUGH g_ascii_formatd(),
                       conversion by conversion, not through
                       g_strdup_printf(): a label must not read "12,3"
                       on a machine with a comma separator, and 80.12's
                       locale case would catch it if it did. The case
                       is there, and skips itself where no comma locale
                       is installed -- which is this machine, so it has
                       been proved by reading rather than by running.
       [x] 80.24 DONE (2026-09-22): THE CODE, part 3 of 4: the node
             object. lib/pn-figure.h plus the GObject half of
             pn-figure.c, and with it the pn-expr-bind extraction
             80.8(c) asked for. Still nothing that draws: the node
             resolves, reports and latches, and 80.25 will stroke.
             Fourteen more cases in tests/unit/test-pn-figure.c (401
             checks in that binary now), valgrind clean, and the whole
             suite of 162 binaries green before and after.
             [x] 80.24.1 DONE: `program`, `inputs`, `background-color`,
                   `font-family`, `stretch`, and the read-only `error`
                   string; the schema puts `program` on a "Figure" tab
                   as a full-width PN_EDITOR_CODE editor highlighted as
                   "sh", with `error` under it as a full-width
                   PN_EDITOR_LABEL, and the three appearance properties
                   on a second tab (80.9h, 80.11b, 80.11c, 80.11e).
                   (a) THE LABEL EDITOR HAD TO LEARN TO BE LIVE, and
                       this is the one thing 80.11(e) did not foresee.
                       build_label_editor() in pn-settings-renderer.c
                       SAMPLED the property once, at dialog build time
                       -- which is exactly wrong for `error`, since
                       80.11(d) has the card and the error text follow
                       every keystroke. A string-typed row now
                       g_object_bind_property()s to the label instead
                       (and wraps, since a parse message is worth
                       reading in full); other types keep the sampled
                       rendering, because g_strdup_value_contents() is
                       not a transform a binding can run. PnFigure is
                       the first PN_EDITOR_LABEL user in the tree, so
                       nothing else changes behaviour.
                   (b) `error` IS READABLE-ONLY, as 80.10(f) decided,
                       and pn-flow.c therefore leaves it out of the
                       saved worksheet. The renderer was checked to
                       make sure it does not drop such a row: it skips
                       construct-only and missing properties, not
                       read-only ones.
             [x] 80.24.2 DONE: sink (has_input TRUE, has_output FALSE),
                   1..8 inputs defaulting to 1, renameable through the
                   dialog's own Inputs tab via
                   pn_node_set_input_count_property ("inputs"), and
                   pn_node_set_collate_inputs (TRUE) (80.8a, 80.8d,
                   80.8g).
             [x] 80.24.3 DONE: receive() clears and refills the
                   snapshot through the new binder, re-resolves once at
                   the at-rest client rectangle purely so the `error`
                   property is right without waiting for a painter
                   (80.10f), and schedules a repaint through the same
                   100 ms coalescing idiom PnPlot and PnOscilloscope
                   keep (80.8h). Nothing is emitted: it is a sink.
                   (a) THE EXTRACTION OF 80.8(c) LANDED HERE, as
                       lib/pn-expr-bind.[ch] (core tier):
                       pn_expr_bind_flat() is Calculator's rule,
                       pn_expr_bind_collated() is Calculator 2's, both
                       feeding a #PnExprBindFunc so the calculators can
                       bind into a #PnVarStore (via the ready-made
                       pn_expr_bind_to_store) while the figure binds
                       into its #PnFigureSnapshot. Both calculators now
                       call it and lost their own copies. The safety
                       net was the one 80.12(g) named: their unit tests
                       pass untouched, before and after.
                   (b) ONE THING THE DESIGN DID NOT KNOW: the core's
                       collation is a NO-OP below two inputs --
                       pn_node_latch_inputs() returns early on n < 2 --
                       so a one-input figure would have seen nothing
                       bound under `value1` at all. pn_expr_bind_collated
                       therefore falls back to the message's own
                       data.value for the ARRIVING input when no
                       collated member is there. For two inputs and up
                       it never fires, since the core has just latched
                       that input, which is why Calculator 2's tests
                       did not move.
             [x] 80.24.4 DONE: class metadata exactly as 80.9(d) spells
                   it (Figure / Sinks / fa-pencil / plum, set in both
                   class_init and init), get_size 280 x 254 with a
                   40 px header and the 210 px client area of 80.9(e),
                   paint_plot_zoom_keep_aspect TRUE (80.4g), and
                   pn_node_factory_register (self, PN_TYPE_FIGURE).
                   get_client_area is NOT overridden: PnNode's
                   geometric default already reports the rectangle
                   under the header, which is the one the worksheet
                   hands paint_plot -- asserted rather than assumed, in
                   the node_client_area case.
             [x] 80.24.5 DONE, with one CORRECTION to 80.11(g). The
                   default program is
                       view 0, 0, 100, 100
                       circle 50, 50, 40
                       text 50, 50, "%.1f", value1
                   -- `value1`, not `value`. The default DISPLAY NAME
                   of input 1 is "value1" (pn_node_get_input_name), and
                   80.8(b) binds under the display name, so `value`
                   would have silently zero-filled forever. Naming the
                   port "value" instead was considered and rejected: it
                   would read value, value2, value3 as soon as anyone
                   raised the input count. Rename the port and the
                   program names it by the new name, which is the
                   workflow the figure actually wants.
       [x] 80.25 DONE (2026-09-22): THE CODE, part 4 of 4: the painter.
             lib/pn-figure-gui.c, gui tier, installed as
             PnNodeClass.paint_plot -- and it stayed DUMB: it walks a
             resolved list and strokes, it evaluates nothing and maps
             nothing. The specimen from the head of #80 draws, and it
             draws like a plate.
             (a) AND IT NEEDED A HARNESS, which is now
                 tools/figure-preview.c, the figure's answer to
                 osc-preview: it renders a program to a PNG through the
                 shipped paint_plot with no GTK window and no editor
                 running, so the styling loop is sub-second and every
                 claim below was looked at rather than reasoned about.
                     ./figure-preview [out.png] [W] [H] [program-file]
                 Default: the reference specimen at the node's own
                 280x210, with the lever angle in PN_FIGURE_A.
             (b) IT FOUND A REAL BUG IN 80.23, and the dump could never
                 have: pn_figure_resolve() set an arc's @negative from
                 the VIEW (k < 0) rather than from the two device
                 angles, so a sweep written BACKWARDS -- 80.6(d)'s own
                 example, `arc 0,0,10,90,0` -- came out as the three
                 quarters going the long way round instead of the
                 clockwise quarter. It is `a1 < a0`, which is what
                 cairo_arc versus cairo_arc_negative actually means.
                 Fixed in core with two new cases (back_arc_sweep,
                 back_arc_reversed) that pin both directions and the
                 reversed-axis pair. Every existing case agreed with
                 both rules, which is exactly why it survived.
             [x] 80.25.1 DONE: save, clip to the rectangle (the
                   worksheet does not clip, 80.4e), and fill it with
                   `background-color` before the program runs, so the
                   letterbox bars read as background (80.4f).
             [x] 80.25.2 DONE: the whole pen state carried off the
                   display list and nothing held by the painter itself,
                   which is what 80.5(g)'s reset buys. Widths and
                   dashes arrive already scaled; `width 0` is the one
                   thing resolved here, through
                   cairo_device_to_user_distance, because "one device
                   pixel however far the worksheet is zoomed in" is a
                   length only the CTM knows (80.5c, 80.5d).
                   fill_preserve-then-stroke, round cap and round join
                   (80.5e, 80.5f).
             [x] 80.25.3 DONE: cairo_arc / cairo_arc_negative on core's
                   own device angles -- see (b) -- inside a
                   save / translate / scale(scale_x, scale_y) /
                   RESTORE-BEFORE-PAINTING, which is the cairo ellipse
                   recipe and the reason the stroke width stays uniform
                   under `stretch` (80.6e). Verified by rendering the
                   same program with stretch off and on.
             [x] 80.25.4 DONE: Pango with
                   pango_font_description_set_absolute_size, the
                   family left UNSET when the property is empty so
                   Pango's own default stands, and the alignment
