[ ] 84 THE DEFERRALS OF FIVE CLOSED ENTRIES, carved out (2026-09-22) so
       they stay visible as work. Each of #57, #59, #60, #62 and #63
       was finished and archived to TODO-DONE with exactly one
       sub-item consciously NOT done; TODO-DONE carries no open boxes,
       so burying five real ideas in it would have lost them. They are
       collected here rather than given five numbers of their own
       because each is a paragraph, not an entry -- and two of them
       (84.2, 84.3) turn out to want the same worksheet.
       Nothing here blocks anything. This entry exists to be READ when
       one of those five nodes is next touched, and to be split into
       its own entry if one of them grows an argument.
       [ ] 84.1 DAILY TIMER (#57.5): no on-canvas indication of the
             current state -- the user wires an LED to see whether the
             schedule is on. A body-colour or header-slider tell (the
             way PnSwitch paints its latch) would save that hop, but
             it needs a gui-tier paint vfunc and, first, a DECISION:
             should a *schedule* look like a *switch*? They are not
             the same thing -- one is driven by the clock and cannot
             be touched, the other is the user's own hand -- and a
             node that looks switchable but is not is worse than a
             node that says nothing.
       [ ] 84.2 BRIDGE QUOTE (#59.8): more bridges -- the enum plus a
             build_request/parse branch per provider is the whole
             cost, now that #60.1 pulled the back-ends out into
             lib/pn-bridge-common.[ch]; a REVERSE quote ("how much PLS
             for 1 BNB"), which is a different question from the one
             the node asks today; and an alert path -- a Comparator on
             data.rate already does it, so what is missing is the
             worked example, which is 84.3.
       [ ] 84.3 BRIDGE CONVERTER (#60.10): a worked example worksheet
             -- Knob -> Bridge Converter -> Numeric, and the two
             providers side by side for one amount, which is the
             comparison #60.9 made by hand on the command line and
             nobody can repeat without rebuilding it. Do this WITH
             84.2: the same sheet answers both, since an alert is one
             Comparator away from the same wire.
       [ ] 84.4 SAVE-DATA ON THE OTHER DISPLAY NODES (#62.7), and this
             one may correctly end as "no". Plot and Oscilloscope hold
             a SNAPSHOT -- the last vector, the last trace -- not the
             accumulated history a Graph holds, so a reload repaints
             them from the next message anyway and the property pair
             would buy nothing. It is worth doing ONLY for the case
             where a user wants the picture to survive a restart with
             no feed running, which is a real case (a plate captured
             once) and a narrow one. Decide before building.
       [ ] 84.5 INTERACTION FOR PLOTS ON THE DESKTOP LAYOUT (#63.10),
             the largest of the five and the only one that needs new
             plumbing. A plot in the desktop window is a PICTURE: the
             Oscilloscope's knobs and PnSunPath's drag-to-rotate do
             nothing there, so a node that is interactive on the
             worksheet silently stops being interactive the moment it
             is placed. The seam exists -- ActivateWidget already
             carries a UUID for Switch and Inject -- and what it needs
             is a COORDINATE-carrying variant plus a per-node "handle
             this click" vfunc, which is an ABI bump (see
             reference_node_class_vfunc_abi). That cost is why this is
             an entry to plan rather than a row to add.

[ ] 85 TWELVE PHYSICS PLATES for the Figure node (#80) -- the teaching
       collection the node was built for, planned here as a LIST and
       nothing more. #80's own header says the point is "worksheets
       that look like the plates in a 19th-century physics book", and
       #80.15 ships exactly two sheets to prove the engine works
       (block and tackle, ray-optics). This entry is the other thing: a dozen
       sheets that are worth opening because of what they TEACH, not
       because of what they test.
       DELIBERATELY NOT DESIGNED YET. Each line below is a subject and
       a sentence, and that is all it is meant to be today. When one is
       picked up it gets its own sub-item with the knobs, the program
       and the physics written out -- twelve designs written in advance
       would be twelve designs written before the node's last verbs
       exist.

       WHAT THIS DEPENDS ON, so nobody starts on the wrong day:
       (a) #80 IS NOT FINISHED. The engine draws (80.22-80.25 are
           done), but 80.13, 80.14, 80.15, 80.19 and 80.21 are open.
       (b) 80.19'S LATER PRIMITIVES GATE SEVERAL OF THESE. The verbs
           today are view, color, width, fill, nofill, move, line,
           rect, circle, arc, poly, dot, text -- there are NO ARROWS,
           no angle-mark arcs, no rotated labels and no hatching. A
           plate of forces without arrowheads is not a plate of
           forces (80.19 says so itself), so the mechanics sheets want
           80.19 first. The wave and circuit ones do not.
       (c) #82's ANIMATION would turn about half of these from a
           picture you wind by hand into a film. Not required for any
           of them; worth knowing before choosing which to build
           first.
       (d) PLACEMENT IS NOT SETTLED. 80.15 ships into
           examples/displays/ because that directory already installs
           and #79.2's Makefile.am gap is still open. Twelve sheets is
           enough to deserve a directory of their own -- but that
           means fixing #79.2 first. Decide when the first one lands,
           not now.
       NOT TO BE CONFUSED WITH: #80.15's two proving sheets (the
       engine's own tests, which stay in #80), and #79's wave-optics
       teaching sheet, which PLOTS intensity curves -- this node draws
       the apparatus, not the curve, and the two are complementary
       rather than competing.

       THE TWELVE, grouped the way a textbook groups them:
       [x] 85.1  DONE (2026-09-22): MECHANICS -- the simple pendulum.
             examples/displays/pendulum.json, one Figure and two knobs,
             and the first of the twelve because it is the one
             MECHANICS plate that does not want 85(b)'s arrows: a
             pendulum is a rod, a bob and an angle, and there is not a
             single force vector on it.
             THE KNOBS, per 80.15.1(a)'s rule that each knob must move
             one visible thing: LENGTH L, 0.5..2.0 m, which stretches
             the rod (drawn 32 units to the metre, so twice as long
             really is twice as long) and moves the period bar;
             RELEASE ANGLE, 0..80 deg, which swings the bob and opens
             the angle mark. The ports are renamed L and theta and the
             program names them (80.8b, 80.24.5); 24 wheel detents
             cross a range, so a notch is 0.0625 m or 3.3 deg.
             THE PHYSICS, and the reason the sheet is worth opening:
             T0 = 2*pi*sqrt(L/g) is the small-angle period, and the
             plate prints the real one beside it,
             T = T0 * (1 + th^2/16 + 11*th^4/3072) -- the first two
             terms of the amplitude series, spelled with
             multiplications because `^` is XOR (80.3e). The whole
             lesson is how LITTLE that correction is: 0.2 % at 10
             degrees, and you must reach 45 before it is 4 %. So the
             period is drawn as a LENGTH on a 0..3.5 s scale at the
             foot of the plate, blue up to T0 and RED for everything
             the angle added -- a sliver that stays invisible however
             hard the reader winds the angle knob, next to a blue bar
             that visibly grows as sqrt(L). The third line of the
             corner readout is printed in the same red, so the number
             and the sliver are obviously the same fact. A fourth line
             says the mass adds nothing, which is the other half of
             the lesson and cannot be drawn at all.
             WHAT THE DRAWING DOES BESIDES: a hatched support (seven
             hand-written strokes -- 80.18's `repeat` is not in v1 and
             this is exactly the demand it deferred), a dotted plumb
             line, the dotted arc the bob actually travels, the far
             extreme drawn hollow so a swing has two ends, and the
             angle mark at the pivot.
             THREE THINGS THE LANGUAGE MADE EASY, worth recording
             because they are what a plate needs from it:
             (a) EVERY AWKWARD CASE IS A clamp(), not a special case.
                 The bob radius is clamp(0.085*Ld, 1.8, 5) so a short
                 pendulum does not become a blob on a stick; the angle
                 mark is min(clamp(0.45*Ld,5,20), 0.75*Ld); and the
                 theta label sits on the bisector but never closer to
                 the vertical than 12 degrees and never further out
                 than the bob, or a small angle on a short rod prints
                 the label straight onto the rod. All of #81's doing --
                 clamp, min and max did not exist when #80 was
                 designed.
             (b) DEGENERATE IS SAFE, and the unwired figure proves it:
                 with both inputs zero-filled (80.2 rule 12) the rod
                 and the bob vanish rather than erroring, and the red
                 line disappears on its own because T/T0 is 0/0 and a
                 non-finite statement is skipped (80.10). A plate that
                 still draws before anything is wired.
             (c) THE BAR IS CLAMPED TO ITS SCALE (Tc = min(T, 3.5)) so
                 a hand-widened knob cannot draw off the plate, while
                 the PRINTED number stays exact. Cheap, and the kind of
                 thing a sheet shipped as an example should get right.
             CHECKED: `./src/pipnode-run -t 3` under
             G_DEBUG=fatal-warnings loads it headless, exits 0, no
             CRITICALs and no warnings; the program was written against
             tools/figure-preview (80.25a) at five knob settings and at
             the node's own 280x210; `make -C examples install
             DESTDIR=...` puts it in place after the automake &&
             ./config.status of 80.15.3.
             PLACEMENT, answering 85(d) for the first one: into
             examples/displays/ with 80.15's sheets, because that
             directory already installs. Twelve sheets still deserve a
             directory of their own -- that is #79.2's fix and it stays
             its own job.
       [ ] 85.2  MECHANICS -- projectile motion. Launch angle and
             speed as knobs; the arc, the apex and the range marked.
       [ ] 85.3  MECHANICS -- the inclined plane. Slope angle as a
             knob; the weight resolved into its two components. Wants
             80.19's arrows and angle marks more than any other sheet
             here.
       [ ] 85.4  GRAVITATION -- Kepler's equal areas. A planet on an
             ellipse with the sun at a focus, and the swept sector
             drawn as the planet moves.
       [ ] 85.5  WAVES -- the standing wave on a string. Harmonic
             number as a knob; nodes and antinodes marked.
       [ ] 85.6  WAVES -- two-source interference, the ripple-tank
             plate. Source separation and wavelength as knobs.
       [ ] 85.7  WAVES -- the Doppler effect. A moving source and the
             wavefronts bunching ahead of it; speed as a knob.
       [ ] 85.8  OPTICS -- the concave mirror. The ray construction
             80.15.2 does for a lens, done for a mirror, which is the
             other half of the same lesson.
       [x] 85.9  DONE (2026-09-22): OPTICS -- refraction and total
             internal reflection. examples/displays/refraction.json,
             one Figure and two knobs, and the plate where the
             LANGUAGE draws the physics: the refracted ray disappears
             past the critical angle because asin() of a sine larger
             than one is not a number, and 80.10(b) skips a statement
             with a non-finite value in it. Nothing in the program
             tests for the critical angle. There is no `if` anywhere
             on the sheet.
             THE KNOBS, one visible thing each (80.15.1a): ANGLE OF
             INCIDENCE, 0..85 deg, swings the ray in the glass and
             opens the refracted one faster than itself; REFRACTIVE
             INDEX n, 1.00..2.50, moves the critical angle, which the
             plate draws as a grey dotted ghost ray so the reader can
             see the threshold before crossing it. The ports are
             renamed theta and n and the program names them (80.8b);
             24 detents make a notch 3.5 deg or 0.0625 of index, and
             the range walks from water (1.33) through glass (1.50) to
             diamond (2.42). 85 rather than 90 on purpose: at exactly
             grazing the Fresnel ratio below is 0/0.
             THE PHYSICS, dense to rare, which is the only direction
             that has a critical angle at all: n sin(th1) = sin(th2),
             th2 = asin(n sin th1), thc = asin(1/n). Then FRESNEL,
             unpolarised -- the mean of the two polarisations -- so
             each ray is drawn AS THICK AS ITS SHARE OF THE LIGHT.
             That is the lesson the angles alone do not teach: at
             normal incidence the reflection is a 4 % hairline lying
             on top of the incident ray, and it swells continuously to
             take the whole beam, so total internal reflection arrives
             as the END of something that was always there rather than
             as a switch thrown at 41.8 degrees.
             cos(th2) IS SPELLED TWICE, ON PURPOSE, and this is the
             whole trick of the sheet: `th2 = asin(s2)` for the
             geometry, which goes NaN past the critical angle and
             takes the refracted ray, its arrowhead, its angle arc,
             its name and the th2 line of the readout out with it; and
             `c2 = sqrt(max(1 - s2*s2, 0))` for the energy, which
             stays real on both sides and reaches zero exactly there,
             which is what drives the Fresnel ratio to one. Same
             quantity, two spellings, because one of them has to
             vanish and the other has to arrive.
             THE EVANESCENT FIELD, and it is the payoff: past the
             critical angle a little hatched plume appears above the
             surface -- the light does not stop dead at the boundary,
             it leaks a fraction of a wavelength into the air and
             comes back. ev = sqrt(s2*s2 - 1) is the magnitude of the
             cosine that turned imaginary, so the plume is NaN and
             therefore absent below the critical angle without being
             asked to be, and the penetration depth d = lambda/(2 pi
             ev) both prints in the readout (406 nm just past thc for
             glass, 43 nm for diamond at 70 deg) and sets the plume's
             HEIGHT, because the strokes are spaced in units of dp.
             #86's `repeat` draws all fourteen of them.
             FOUND BY WRITING IT, and it is a language fact rather
             than a plate fact: `max` and `min` are fmax/fmin and
             SWALLOW A NaN -- max(NaN, x) is x -- while `clamp`
             propagates it, deliberately (lib/pn-expr-funcs.c checks
             isnan first). So a label that must vanish with its
             quantity has to be floored with clamp, not max: the first
             draft's theta-2 label was clamped with max and went on
             printing itself next to a ray that no longer existed.
             The rule worth remembering: clamp is the NaN-safe floor,
             max is the NaN-eating one, and 80.10(b)'s "degenerate is
             safe" only works through the first.
             WHAT THE DRAWING DOES BESIDES: arrowheads, hand-built out
             of `poly` from each ray's own direction the way 85.12
             built its needles, because 85(b)'s arrows are still
             80.19's job and a ray diagram without a direction is not
             one; the two angle marks below the surface drawn as arcs
             and NOT lettered, so the law of reflection is shown
             rather than asserted; the rays NAMED instead, each label
             placed just past the end of its own ray and pushed down
             by 8*sin^2(theta) so a grazing ray does not write its
             name across the surface; and the media labelled at the
             left edge, which the geometry guarantees is empty -- the
             incident ray is always in the lower left, the reflected
             always lower right, the refracted always x >= 0.
             SIZED FOR THE CARD, NOT FOR THE ZOOM, and this is the
             first of the three plates to be: the first draft used a
             160-unit view and font 3.8 for the readout, which is 6.6
             DEVICE PIXELS in the node's own 280x210 client area, and
             the user said so immediately. 80.4(b) makes every length
             scale with the view, so legibility at rest is one number
             -- the view's width -- against the text sizes. The fix
             was a 132-unit view (s = 2.12 px/unit) and font 4.8 for
             the readout, ten pixels, and it cost a line: the "each
             ray is drawn as thick as its share of the light"
             footnote went to the sheet's Comment node, because a
             caption too small to read is not a caption. The rule for
             85.2-85.11: pick the view width so the smallest text you
             mean anyone to read is about ten device pixels at
             280x210, and budget the plate's words against that
             BEFORE drawing, not after. Both earlier plates are
             smaller than this one at rest and are candidates for the
             same pass.
             THE CRITICAL RAY IS NOT LETTERED, which the resizing
             forced and which turned out to be the better answer:
             near thc the ghost ray and the incident ray ARE the same
             ray, so any label on it lands on an orange rod. Knocking
             the letters out of the drawing with a background-filled
             rect was tried and rejected on sight -- it cuts the
             incident ray in two and reads as a broken ray, not as a
             label. So the ghost carries no letters at all and is
             tied to its number by COLOUR: the dotted ray and the thc
             line of the readout are the same grey, which is exactly
             85.1's red-sliver trick (the number and the mark are
             obviously one fact) used for the second time.
             DEGENERATE IS SAFE, as always: unwired both inputs are
             zero-filled (80.2 rule 12), 1/n is infinite, asin of it
             is NaN, and the critical-angle line of the readout simply
             does not print while the rest of the plate still draws.
             CHECKED: `./src/pipnode-run -t 3` under
             G_DEBUG=fatal-warnings loads it headless, exits 0, no
             CRITICALs and no warnings; written against
             tools/figure-preview at fourteen knob settings -- both
             ends of both knobs, either side of the critical angle,
             and unwired -- and read at the node's own 280x210 beside
             pendulum.json and bar-magnet.json rendered the same way,
             which is how the type sizes were chosen; in a live
             editor the figure reports an empty `error`, two inputs,
             two wires and port names that round-trip as theta/n.
             Installs beside pendulum.json and bar-magnet.json in
             examples/displays (85.1's placement answer, unchanged).
       [ ] 85.10 HEAT -- the Carnot cylinder. Piston position through
             the four strokes, the state labelled at each corner.
             Named in #80's own header as a thing the node should be
             able to draw.
       [ ] 85.11 ELECTRICITY -- the Wheatstone bridge. Three known
             resistances as knobs, the galvanometer needle answering,
             and balance shown rather than asserted. Also named in
             #80's header.
       [x] 85.12 DONE (2026-09-22): MAGNETISM -- the field of a bar
             magnet. examples/displays/bar-magnet.json, forty compass
             needles placed by ONE `repeat` block, which is the plate
             that asked for #86 and the reason that entry exists.
             THE KNOBS, one visible thing each (80.15.1a): MAGNET
             BEARING, 0..360 deg, turns the bar and the whole field
             turns with it; EARTH FIELD, 0..8, adds a uniform field up
             the page. At 0 the sheet is the textbook bar-magnet plate
             on its own; wind it up and the far needles stop caring
             about the magnet and all point north, which is what a
             compass on a real table actually does.
             THE PHYSICS: two poles, +p and -p, sitting at +/- d along
             the magnet's axis, each falling off as 1/r^2, summed with
             the uniform field, and the needle laid along the sum
             (atan2, which #81 made available). Two poles rather than
             an ideal dipole on purpose -- it is what a bar magnet IS
             in the plate tradition, and it keeps the near field
             visibly different from the far one.
             THE NEUTRAL POINTS, and this is the sheet's real lesson:
             each needle is drawn AS LONG AS THE FIELD IT STANDS IN,
             clamped at both ends, so the places where the magnet's
             field exactly cancels the earth's show up as a limp pair
             that walks inwards as the earth field grows. With the
             magnet's north up they sit on the equator at
             r = (2*p*d/E)^(1/3) -- and nothing in the program computes
             that: the grid FINDS them, which is the whole argument for
             drawing a field as needles instead of as a formula.
             THE GRID STRADDLES THE MAGNET: eight columns at +/- 12,
             24, 36, 48 and five rows at 0 and +/- 18, 36, so the middle
             row runs along the equator where the interesting thing
             happens and no needle ever lands on the bar. That is what
             the column formula's sign trick is for --
             (12 + 12 * (k - 4 * floor(k / 4))) * (2 * floor(k / 4) - 1)
             -- and it is the concrete answer to 86.2's claim that one
             loop plus floor arithmetic is enough for a grid.
             WHAT IT COST THE LANGUAGE: #86 and nothing else. No arrows
             (85b) and no rotated labels: N and S are letters placed at
             POINTS that rotate with the bar, which sidesteps 80.7(h)
             rather than solving it.
             CHECKED: `./src/pipnode-run -t 3` under
             G_DEBUG=fatal-warnings loads it headless, exits 0, no
             CRITICALs and no warnings; written against
             tools/figure-preview at seven knob settings and at the
             node's own 280x210; in a live editor the figure reports
             an empty `error`, two inputs, two wires, and its port
             names round-trip as bearing/earth. Installs beside
             pendulum.json in examples/displays (85.1's placement
             answer, unchanged).

[x] 86 DONE (2026-09-22): a `repeat` BLOCK for the figure language.
       80.18(a), built, because 85.12 is the "something real" that
       80.18(c) said to wait for. A field of compass needles is
       thirty-odd needles that differ only in where they stand; without
       a loop the program is the same five lines copied thirty times,
       which is not a plate anyone would read in the node's own dialog.
       The block changes no engine and no ABI: it is a front-end stage
       and a jump in the resolver's walk.
       The FORM was decided in 80.18(a) and is not reopened here; what
       this entry adds is what that form comes to in code, and the two
       or three questions 80.18 did not have to answer because nothing
       was being built.
       [x] 86.1 THE FORM, as 80.18(a) settled it: `repeat <count>` ...
             `end`, with the block binding a scalar index. Two things
             that entry left open, decided here:
             (a) THE COUNT IS AN EXPRESSION like every other argument,
                 not a literal -- `repeat cols * rows` is the natural
                 way to write a grid, and the folding of 80.3(b) makes
                 a constant count free anyway.
             (b) THE INDEX IS ALWAYS `i`, not a name the block chooses.
                 One form is one paragraph in the help and one thing to
                 remember; a `repeat n as k` spelling buys nothing while
                 nesting is refused (86.2). An input or an assignment
                 called `i` is SHADOWED inside the block, and the help
                 says so.
       [x] 86.2 NO NESTING, and that is the whole of 80.2 rule 6's
             "80.18 gets to revisit that exactly once": a `repeat`
             inside a `repeat` is a parse error with a line. A grid --
             the actual demand -- is one loop and the floor/mod
             arithmetic that #81 made cheap, `gx = i - cols *
             floor(i / cols)`. Refusing nesting keeps the matching a
             single forward scan and keeps the index question (86.1b)
             from needing an answer at all. It can be lifted later
             without changing what a program that works today means.
       [x] 86.3 A NEW FRONT-END STAGE, pn_figure_check_blocks(), between
             the verb table and the literals: the verbs must be known
             before the structure can be read, and nothing after it
             should have to ask whether the block it is standing in
             closes. It reports `end` without `repeat`, `repeat`
             without `end`, and `repeat` inside `repeat` -- each with
             its line, each collected rather than fatal, so a program
             with two of them still says how many there were (80.2 rule
             9). A structural error makes the program draw nothing, like
             every other program error (80.10a).
             FOUND BY WRITING IT: the stage counts DEPTH rather than
             holding one "a block is open" flag, and the difference is
             not cosmetic. With a flag, a nested `repeat` is reported,
             then the `end` that closes it is reported AGAIN as an
             `end` with nothing open -- one mistake, two messages, and
             the count in "2 errors, first on line 2" lies about how
             many things are wrong. Depth reports the nesting once and
             lets the rest of the program balance.
       [x] 86.4 THE INDEX BINDS LIKE ANY OTHER NAME, which is what makes
             it cost nothing: the resolver sets `i` in the frame's store
             at the top of each iteration, and 80.2 rule 12's zero-fill
             cannot fight it because a binding wins over a zero-fill and
             the loop rebinds every pass. An argument inside the block
             that mentions `i` therefore cannot fold (it reads a
             variable), and one that does not -- a `width`, a colour --
             folds exactly as it would outside. No new machinery on
             either path.
       [x] 86.5 A COUNT IS A VALUE, NOT A PROGRAM (80.10b): zero,
             negative, NaN or infinite means the block does not run, and
             leaves ONE skip marker saying why rather than reddening the
             node -- a knob winding through zero must not latch an
             error. The count truncates toward zero. And it is CAPPED:
             above PN_FIGURE_MAX_REPEAT (1000 iterations) the block is
             skipped whole, with the reason `too-many` in the dump,
             because a figure that freezes the editor for a mistyped
             exponent is worse than one that refuses out loud. A vector
             count is 80.10(c) like every other vector argument, until
             #82.
       [x] 86.6 PEN STATE AND ASSIGNMENTS ARE SEQUENTIAL ACROSS
             ITERATIONS, deliberately: the block is shorthand for
             writing the statements out n times, not a scope. A `color`
             set inside the block survives into the next iteration and
             out the far side, exactly as the copied-out lines would.
             The alternative -- saving and restoring the pen around each
             pass -- would make the block the only construct in the
             language with a scope, and 80.5(i) has not even given us
             push/pop yet.
       [x] 86.7 NO OPERATION OF ITS OWN in the display list, for the
             same reason an assignment leaves none: control flow is not
             ink. The dump shows each iteration's operations carrying
             the SAME source line numbers, which is exactly what makes a
             loop assertable -- three `line` ops from line 4 IS the
             loop having run three times.
       [x] 86.8 THE TESTS, in tests/unit/test-pn-figure.c beside the
             rest: the block runs n times with `i` counting from 0; a
             zero and a negative count draw nothing and say so; a
             non-finite count skips; the cap refuses; `end` without
             `repeat`, `repeat` without `end` and a nested `repeat` are
             parse errors with their lines; pen state and assignments
             carry across iterations; a folded argument inside a block
             is still folded; and one real grid, because the arithmetic
             85.12 depends on deserves a case of its own.
       [x] 86.9 WHAT IT DOES NOT DO, so nobody reads more into it:
             no nesting (86.2), no `break` and no conditional --
             `if(c,a,b)` is a function in the expression language and
             a zero radius already skips a statement (80.6i), which
             between them cover what a plate needs. And 80.18(b)'s
             invariant stands unchanged: INSIDE a repeat a vector is
             still a FRAME, never an iteration, and the index is
             always a scalar. #82 must
             not quietly reinterpret it.
       [x] 86.10 THE DOCUMENTATION: the `program` property blurb lists
             the verbs and grows `repeat`/`end` now, since it is what
             the dialog shows. The language SPECIFICATION is 80.14's
             page, which still does not exist -- so 80.14's contents
             list grows the repeat paragraph and 80.18(b)'s invariant
             rather than this entry inventing a second home for them.

[ ] 87 The Figure KEEPS ITS EVALUATED FILM between frames -- asked for by a
       double-pendulum example, which integrates the motion in a
       `repeat 1000` loop and cost ~95 ms a frame, because every paint
       re-ran the whole program over the whole film only to pick out one
       element of each argument.
       [x] 87.1 pn_figure_resolve() SPLIT IN TWO: pn_figure_trace_new()
             walks the program once with the vectors in place and records
             every drawing statement with its evaluated arguments (a
             `repeat` body once per pass, assignments not at all);
             pn_figure_trace_draw() turns frame k of that walk into a
             display list without evaluating anything. pn_figure_resolve()
             is now the two back to back, so every existing test still
             asserts the same output.
       [x] 87.2 THE NODE KEEPS THE TRACE and throws it away on the four
             things that change it: a program edit (whose statements the
             trace points into), a new message (the snapshot), `frames`
             and `play-mode` (the layout of `t`). It does NOT depend on
             the rectangle, so the zoom overlay draws from it too.
       [x] 87.3 A VECTOR `repeat` COUNT walks a different number of passes
             in each frame, so a trace that met one is good for that one
             frame only and is re-walked per frame -- the old cost, for
             the programs that need it and no others.
       [x] 87.4 ERROR ORDER KEPT: a frame's own error (an empty vector, a
             frame past a vector's end) comes before a later evaluation
             failure, as it did when the walk stopped at the first one.
       [x] 87.5 VERIFIED: the double pendulum's 250 frames dump byte-for-
             byte the same before and after; a frame now costs 0.7 ms
             instead of ~95 ms. Tests (kept_film_*) build the final state
             on a fresh node and compare, for input, program, frames,
             play-mode, a vector repeat count and a second rectangle.
             Dropping the program-edit invalidation is a use-after-free
             that the plain test can miss by luck; valgrind catches it.
       [ ] 87.6 A NEW MESSAGE STILL COSTS ONE FULL WALK, on receive (for
             the `error` property, 80.10f): ~90 ms for the pendulum, so a
             knob spun fast over it is sluggish. Deferring the walk to
             the next paint would need the error judged there instead.
       [x] 87.7 THE DOUBLE-PENDULUM EXAMPLE, examples/displays/double-
             pendulum.json: two knobs set the arms' LENGTHS (0.3-1.5;
             they started as the starting angles, but lengths change the
             motion more visibly), both arms start at 120/150 deg, a
             `repeat 1000` loop steps the equations (semi-implicit Euler,
             dt 0.01 s, four steps a frame, 250 frames = 10 s, loop), the
             lower bob leaves a fading three-second track, and a grey
             ghost started 0.01 deg further out shows the chaos. ~190 ms
             per knob change (two pendulums), 0.8 ms per frame.
             PnFigure.html's Animation section grew "What it costs".

[ ] 88 FIGURES WITH A CONTROLLER IN THE LOOP -- every Figure example so
       far is knobs wired straight into a figure.  This entry is the
       next shape: the knobs go to a Calculator 2 that DECIDES something,
       and the figure draws the decision, so the plate sits in an
       equilibrium (or a named special state) however the knobs move.
       [x] 88.1 DONE (2026-09-26): displays/tower-crane.json -- a tower
             crane and its load-moment controller.  Knobs LOAD m 0..10 t
             and REACH r 6..42 m feed a Calculator 2 (inputs m, r) that
             puts a 12 t counterweight on its 4..15 m track where the
             moments about the mast cancel, and when the track runs out
             refuses the rest of the reach: rmax = (M xmax + Wc 7 -
             Wj 22)/(m + mt), a hyperbola in m, i.e. the crane's load
             chart.  The figure draws only what the controller sends:
             moments printed equal, cab lamp green; past the limit the
             trolley stops at the red rmax mark on the jib, a dotted
             ghost keeps the asked-for reach, the counterweight rests on
             a red stop and the lamp turns red.  No `if`: the red marks
             divide a coordinate by `limited` or (rmax < rtip).
             (a) FIVE INPUTS, ONE PER DECISION (reworked 2026-09-26 at
                 the user's request, so the figure no longer reads like
                 the display of one big calculator).  The controller is
                 three small nodes: LOAD CHART (a Calculator, m -> rmax),
                 REACH LIMITER (Calculator 2, r and rmax -> reach) and
                 COUNTERWEIGHT (Calculator 2, m and reach -> x).  The
                 figure takes m, r, rmax, reach, x on five named inputs
                 and works out `limited` and the two moments itself, so
                 the equal moments are its own check on the controller.
                 WHY EACH NUMBER IS A HEADLINE VALUE: the first build
                 sent everything as sibling members of one controller
                 message (read as m1, reach1, ...).  That only works on
                 one wire -- the Figure clears its snapshot per message
                 and binds siblings only from the message that just
                 arrived (pn_expr_bind_collated rule 2), whereas the
                 core latches each input's data.value.  One value per
                 wire is what makes a multi-input figure robust.  The
                 cost: the crane data (M, mt, Wj, Wc, track) is repeated
                 in the calculators and the figure, because the
                 calculator language cannot read document globals yet.
             (b) THE CALCULATOR LANGUAGE HAS NO `#` COMMENTS -- only the
                 Figure's scanner strips them.  The first draft of the
                 controller was commented and failed with "unexpected
                 character '#'"; its explanation lives in the sheet's
                 Comment node instead.  Worth a sub-item of its own if
                 controllers like this multiply: comments in
                 Calculator/Calculator 2 expressions.
             (c) CHECKED: tools/figure-preview at six (m, r) settings
                 either side of the limit; pipnode-run -t 3 under
                 G_DEBUG=fatal-warnings exits 0; in a throwaway editor
                 the controller's output at (8, 30) is reach 14.65,
                 x 15, limited 1, Mfront = Mback = 190.5, and the
                 figure's `error` is empty at every setting; installs
                 via make -C examples install DESTDIR=...
       [x] 88.2 DONE (2026-09-26): displays/wheatstone-bridge.json -- the
             self-balancing bridge, as the temperature recorder it
             historically was.  A Pt100 (100 ohm at 0 C, alpha 0.00385)
             sits in the unknown arm Rx.  Knobs TEMPERATURE T -50..450 C
             and RATIO ARM R1 50..200 ohm (R2 fixed at 100).  Three
             calculators, one job each, one headline per wire (88.1a):
             PT100 SENSOR (T -> Rx), SERVO (Rx, R1 -> R3 = R1 Rx / R2,
             clamped to the rheostat's 0..400 ohm) and READING (R3, R1
             -> Tm, the temperature read back off R3 -- all a real
             instrument can see).  The five-input figure (T, R1, Rx, R3,
             Tm) decides nothing: it works out the galvanometer current
             from the whole circuit by Thevenin across B-D (2 V battery,
             Rg 50 ohm, 50 uA full scale) and swings the needle from it.
             EQUILIBRIUM: the blue pointer climbs the rheostat's dial,
             the reading tracks T exactly and the needle sits on zero.
             SPECIAL STATE: turning R1 up shrinks the range; once R3
             needs more than 400 ohm the pointer rests on a red stop,
             the needle deflects in red, "off the end of R3" prints and
             the reading sticks at the range's top (259.7 C at R1 200).
             No `if`: the red marks divide a coordinate by `off` or by
             (R3 >= R3max).  Resistor boxes on the 45-degree arms are
             `poly`s with their corners resolved onto the axes.
             CHECKED: tools/figure-preview at six (T, R1) settings;
             pipnode-run -t 3 under G_DEBUG=fatal-warnings exits 0; in a
             throwaway editor (265, 200) gives Rx 202.0, R3 400, Tm
             259.7 and (-50, 50) gives R3 40.4, Tm -50.0, with the
             figure's `error` empty throughout; installs via
             make -C examples install DESTDIR=...
             Not a replacement for 85.11, which is the MANUAL bridge
             (three resistances as knobs, balance found by hand); this
             sheet is its automatic sibling.
       [ ] 88.3 Candidate for the next one: an artillery piece that aims
             itself at a knob-placed target (85.2 with the elevation
             solved, falling short at 45 deg when out of range).
